Available string encodings must be an array, not nil.

+[NSString availableStringEncodings] returned nil behind a TODO. The contract is a pointer to a
ZERO TERMINATED array, and callers walk it with no null check because the documentation promises
one, so nil is not a missing feature, it is a guaranteed crash in correct calling code.

iTerm2 does exactly that, and it is right to:

  callq  *objc_msgSend      ; +[NSString availableStringEncodings]
  movq   (%rax), %rdx       ; SIGSEGV on the nil this used to return
  testq  %rdx, %rdx         ; the zero terminator it was walking towards

That is -[ProfilesTerminalPreferencesViewController sortedEncodings] +0x42, reached from
awakeFromNib at object 922 of 3104 of the Preferences nib. The process died with signal 11 while
the harness reported cider-app exit=0, which is why it read for a long time as a nib that simply
stopped.

THE LIST IS ONLY WHAT THIS PORT CAN CONVERT. A caller asks so it can offer the user a choice, so
advertising an encoding whose conversion is not implemented only moves the failure to the moment
the user picks it. These ten are the ones named by the conversion paths in NSString.m.

localizedNameOfStringEncoding: returned nil too, and the same caller asks for one name per entry to
label its menu. It now answers for every encoding the list advertises, plus the common ones it does
not. The names are English: there are no localization tables here, and a name is a better answer
than none.

MEASURED, and this is the last of three defects between Command comma and a window. The Preferences
nib awake walk reached object 811 before any of this, 922 with the first two fixed, and 3103 of
3104 with this one. PreferencePanel prints loadNibFile leave and done for the first time,
-[PreferencePanel run] reaches showWindow: and the trace reads ENTER, HAVE-WINDOW, ORDERED, and the
Settings window is on screen with its eight toolbar items and its tab row.

--- a/src/NSString.m
+++ b/src/NSString.m
@@ -907,16 +907,71 @@
     return NSISOLatin1StringEncoding;
 }
 
+/*
+ * NIL HERE IS A GUARANTEED CRASH IN THE CALLER, NOT A MISSING FEATURE.
+ *
+ * The contract is a pointer to a ZERO TERMINATED array, and callers walk it without a null check
+ * because the documentation promises one. iTerm2 does exactly that, and it is correct to:
+ *
+ *   callq  *objc_msgSend          ; +[NSString availableStringEncodings]
+ *   movq   (%rax), %rdx           ; <- SIGSEGV on the nil this used to return
+ *   testq  %rdx, %rdx             ; the zero terminator it was walking towards
+ *
+ * That is -[ProfilesTerminalPreferencesViewController sortedEncodings] +0x42, and it killed iTerm2
+ * outright during its Preferences nib decode, at object 922 of 3104. The process died with signal
+ * 11 while the harness reported exit=0.
+ *
+ * THE LIST IS ONLY WHAT THIS PORT CAN ACTUALLY CONVERT, which is the point of the call: a caller
+ * asks so it can offer the user a choice, and advertising an encoding whose conversion is not
+ * implemented moves the failure to where the user picks it. These are the encodings named by the
+ * conversion paths in this file.
+ */
 + (const NSStringEncoding *)availableStringEncodings
 {
-#warning TODO https://code.google.com/p/apportable/issues/detail?id=269
-    return nil;
+    static const NSStringEncoding encodings[] = {
+        NSASCIIStringEncoding,
+        NSUTF8StringEncoding,
+        NSISOLatin1StringEncoding,
+        NSISOLatin2StringEncoding,
+        NSUnicodeStringEncoding,
+        NSWindowsCP1250StringEncoding,
+        NSWindowsCP1251StringEncoding,
+        NSWindowsCP1252StringEncoding,
+        NSWindowsCP1253StringEncoding,
+        NSWindowsCP1254StringEncoding,
+        0
+    };
+
+    return encodings;
 }
 
+/*
+ * A NAME FOR EVERY ENCODING THE LIST ABOVE ADVERTISES, because the same caller asks for one per
+ * entry to label its menu, and nil there is an empty row at best. Not localized: there are no
+ * tables for it here, and an English name is a better answer than none.
+ */
 + (NSString *)localizedNameOfStringEncoding:(NSStringEncoding)encoding
 {
-#warning TODO https://code.google.com/p/apportable/issues/detail?id=269
-    return nil;
+    switch (encoding) {
+        case NSASCIIStringEncoding:         return @"Western (ASCII)";
+        case NSUTF8StringEncoding:          return @"Unicode (UTF-8)";
+        case NSISOLatin1StringEncoding:     return @"Western (ISO Latin 1)";
+        case NSISOLatin2StringEncoding:     return @"Central European (ISO Latin 2)";
+        case NSUnicodeStringEncoding:       return @"Unicode (UTF-16)";
+        case NSWindowsCP1250StringEncoding: return @"Central European (Windows Latin 2)";
+        case NSWindowsCP1251StringEncoding: return @"Cyrillic (Windows)";
+        case NSWindowsCP1252StringEncoding: return @"Western (Windows Latin 1)";
+        case NSWindowsCP1253StringEncoding: return @"Greek (Windows)";
+        case NSWindowsCP1254StringEncoding: return @"Turkish (Windows Latin 5)";
+        case NSNonLossyASCIIStringEncoding: return @"Non-lossy ASCII";
+        case NSMacOSRomanStringEncoding:    return @"Western (Mac OS Roman)";
+        case NSUTF16BigEndianStringEncoding:    return @"Unicode (UTF-16BE)";
+        case NSUTF16LittleEndianStringEncoding: return @"Unicode (UTF-16LE)";
+        case NSUTF32StringEncoding:             return @"Unicode (UTF-32)";
+        case NSUTF32BigEndianStringEncoding:    return @"Unicode (UTF-32BE)";
+        case NSUTF32LittleEndianStringEncoding: return @"Unicode (UTF-32LE)";
+        default:                            return nil;
+    }
 }
 
 + (id)string
